              "id": "4b450103-75f3-393f-82c0-82eb64c05175",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"File Permission Vulnerability with Chmod 0o755 on ./scripts/configure.sh\" in Python programming language refers to the insecure permission setting for the file `configure.sh`. The `os.chmod` function is used to change the permissions of a file or directory. The `0o755` permission allows the owner of the file to read, write, and execute the file, while others can only read and execute it. This could potentially allow unauthorized users to execute the script, leading to a security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should restrict the permissions of the file to only those necessary. If the file does not need to be executed by others, you should remove the execute permission for others. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```python\nimport os\n\n# Change the permission to 0o700, which only allows the owner to read, write, and execute the file\nos.chmod(\"./scripts/configure.sh\", 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` library, which is a built-in library in Python and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "421a7a7a-43ec-3096-8594-f79c57174f27",
              "name": "resources.rules.raptor-signed-unsigned-conversion",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Opengrep Finding: resources.rules.raptor-signed-unsigned-conversion"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The software uses a signed primitive and performs a cast to an unsigned primitive, or uses an unsigned primitive and performs a cast to a signed primitive, which can produce an unexpected value. When the result of a function is to be used as a size parameter, using negative return values can have unexpected results. Although less frequent an issue, unsigned-to-signed conversion can be  the precursor to buffer underwrite conditions. Buffer underwrites  occur frequently when large unsigned values are cast to signed values,  and then used as indexes into a buffer or for pointer arithmetic."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "enabled": true,
                "level": "warning"
              },
