            {
              "id": "4d48a5b4-c015-31f3-827b-313b1704eb17",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `os.chmod(makeall, 0o755)` function in Python sets the permissions of the file specified by `makeall` to `755` (read, write, and execute permissions for the owner, and read and execute permissions for the group and others). This can be a security vulnerability if the file contains sensitive data or functionality, as it allows any user on the system to read and execute the file.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Limit the permissions of files to only what is necessary for your application to function.\n- Avoid setting execute permissions for all users unless absolutely necessary.\n- Be especially cautious when setting permissions on files that contain sensitive data or functionality.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of setting the permissions to `755`, you could set them to `700` (read, write, and execute permissions for the owner only), or another more restrictive set of permissions that still allows your application to function. For example:\n\n```python\nos.chmod(makeall, 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` module, which is part of the Python Standard Library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
