            {
              "id": "7653d81e-aa70-3e71-bedf-9bad12774698",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability arises from the use of the `os.chmod` function in Python to set a permissive mask of `0o775` on the `bin/figtree` file. This permission setting allows the owner and the group to read, write, and execute the file, while others can only read and execute it. This could potentially allow unauthorized users to modify or execute the file, leading to a security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to follow the principle of least privilege. This means that you should only grant the minimum permissions necessary for a user to perform their tasks. In this case, you might want to consider setting a less permissive mask, such as `0o755`, which would allow the owner to read, write, and execute the file, but only allow the group and others to read and execute it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nos.chmod(\"bin/figtree\", 0o755)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` library, which is a standard library in Python and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "49ecb5fe-39bd-314f-a987-38ad0f1a84de",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"File Vulnerability due to Chmod Permissive Mask 0o755 Setting\" in Python refers to the insecure permission setting for a file. The `os.chmod` function is used to change the permissions of a file or directory. The permission mask `0o755` means that the owner has read, write, and execute permissions, while the group and others have only read and execute permissions. This can be a security risk if the file contains sensitive data or is a critical system file, as it allows any user to read and execute the file.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should restrict the permissions of the file to only those users who need access. This can be done by setting a more restrictive permission mask. For example, you could use the permission mask `0o700` to give only the owner read, write, and execute permissions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed version of the code:\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom os.path import join as join_path\n\nos.chmod(join_path(self.stage.source_path, \"linux_x64_64_sfx.exe\"), 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following Python standard library modules:\n\n- `os`\n- `os.path`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "13ed3a7f-0aeb-3ad9-9e75-0ab4b03d59a8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability arises when a Python script uses the `os.chmod` function to set the permissions of a file to `0o755`. This permission setting allows the owner of the file to read, write, and execute the file, while other users can only read and execute it. If the file contains sensitive data or operations, this could lead to unauthorized access or execution of the file by other users on the same system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should carefully consider the necessary permissions for each file in your application. Avoid giving unnecessary permissions, especially write and execute permissions, to users who do not need them. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of setting the permissions to `0o755`, you could set them to `0o700` to only allow the owner of the file to read, write, and execute it:\n\n```python\nos.chmod(perm_script, 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example only requires the built-in `os` module in Python, so no additional library dependencies are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
