            {
              "id": "51ed3c68-ec29-39fe-ade3-32b6a85d3881",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"File Vulnerability due to Chmod Permissive Mask 0o755 Setting\" in Python refers to the insecure permission setting for a file. The `os.chmod` function is used to change the permissions of a file. The permission mask `0o755` means that the owner of the file has read, write, and execute permissions, while the group and others have only read and execute permissions. This can be a security risk if the file contains sensitive data or if it's a critical system file, as it allows any user to read and execute the file.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should restrict the permissions of the file to only what is necessary. If the file does not need to be executed by other users, you should remove the execute permission for the group and others. If the file does not need to be read by other users, you should also remove the read permission for the group and others.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nYou can fix the vulnerability in the provided code by changing the permission mask to `0o700`, which gives the owner read, write, and execute permissions, but gives no permissions to the group and others:\n\n```python\nos.chmod(join_path(self.stage.source_path, \"configure\"), 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` library, which is included in the standard Python library, so no additional dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
