            {
              "id": "54a286cd-e5af-30be-a7d5-921050b58217",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn Python, the `chmod` function is used to change the permissions of a file or a directory. The `0o755` permission setting allows the owner of the file to read, write, and execute the file, while others can only read and execute it. This can lead to a file vulnerability if the file contains sensitive information or functionality. An attacker with read or execute permissions can exploit this vulnerability to gain unauthorized access or control.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Limit the permissions of the file to only what is necessary for its function.\n- Avoid using permissive chmod masks like `0o755` for sensitive files.\n- Regularly review and update the permissions of your files to ensure they are secure.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using a permissive chmod mask like `0o755`, you should use a more restrictive mask like `0o700` which only allows the owner of the file to read, write, and execute it. Here is how you can change the chmod mask in your code:\n\n```python\nchmod(join_path(self.stage.source_path, \"configure\"), 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies to execute properly:\n\n- `os` (for the `chmod` function and `join_path` function)\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
