              "id": "62844f4f-837c-3045-80e1-7b11f4382daf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `os.chmod(perm_script_path, 0o755)` function in Python is used to change the permissions of a file or directory. The `0o755` permission setting allows the owner of the file to read, write, and execute the file, while others can only read and execute it. This can lead to a file permission vulnerability if the file contains sensitive data or functionality, as it allows any user to read and execute the file.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should limit the permissions of the file to only those users who need access. This can be done by setting the permissions to `0o700`, which only allows the owner of the file to read, write, and execute it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can change the permissions of the file to `0o700`:\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nos.chmod(perm_script_path, 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` library, which is a built-in library in Python and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
