              "id": "14e070b0-45be-3ec8-a595-dd5880a9e75a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability arises from the use of the `os.chmod(viewer_file, 0o755)` function in Python, which sets the permissions of the `viewer_file` to `0o755`. This means that the file is readable and executable by everyone, and writable by the owner of the file. This could potentially allow an attacker to read sensitive information from the file or execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should limit the permissions of the file to only what is necessary. For example, if the file does not need to be executable, you should not set the executable permission. If the file does not need to be readable by everyone, you should not set the readable permission for everyone.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of `os.chmod(viewer_file, 0o755)`, you could use `os.chmod(viewer_file, 0o700)` to make the file only readable, writable, and executable by the owner.\n\n```python\nimport os\n\n# Incorrect: Sets the permissions of viewer_file to 0o755\nos.chmod(viewer_file, 0o755)\n\n# Correct: Sets the permissions of viewer_file to 0o700\nos.chmod(viewer_file, 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` library, which is included in the standard Python library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
