            {
              "id": "d21ee61f-7f3b-38dc-99f8-56758620007e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Shell Process Initiation: Potential Injection Vulnerability Detected\" vulnerability in Python refers to the potential for an attacker to inject malicious code into the system command that is being executed. This is possible when user input is used to construct a system command, as in the provided code example. If an attacker can control the `test_bin(\"kmetis\")` or `graph` variables, they could potentially inject arbitrary commands to be executed on the system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using user input to construct system commands whenever possible. If it is necessary to use user input in this way, make sure to properly sanitize and validate the input to ensure that it does not contain any malicious code. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer way to execute system commands in Python is to use the `subprocess` module, which allows you to pass command arguments as a list. This ensures that each argument is treated as a literal string and not interpreted by the shell. Here's how you could rewrite the provided code example:\n\n```python\nimport subprocess\n\nsubprocess.run([test_bin(\"kmetis\"), graph, '5'])\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code example does not appear to require any specific library dependencies beyond the standard Python library.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B605",
                  "d21ee61f-7f3b-38dc-99f8-56758620007e"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "366fba3b-0c7b-393f-a5bc-d143c3fef574",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Shell Process Initiation: Potential Injection Vulnerability Detected\" vulnerability in Python refers to the potential for an attacker to inject malicious code into the system command being executed by the Python script. This is possible when user input or other untrusted data is used to construct a system command without proper sanitization or validation. In the provided code example, the `os.system()` function is used to execute a system command that includes user-provided or untrusted data (`test_bin(\"partnmesh\")` and `graph`).\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `os.system()` or similar functions that execute shell commands with user-provided or untrusted data. If it's necessary to execute system commands, use safer alternatives like `subprocess.run()` or `subprocess.Popen()`, which do not use the shell implicitly and are not susceptible to shell injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using `subprocess.run()`:\n\n```python\nimport subprocess\n\nsubprocess.run([test_bin(\"partnmesh\"), graph, '10'], check=True)\n```\n\nIn this version, the command and its arguments are provided as a list of strings, which are not processed by the shell.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following Python standard library:\n\n- `subprocess`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B605",
                  "366fba3b-0c7b-393f-a5bc-d143c3fef574"
                ]
              }
            },
