            {
              "id": "2c6132c8-1fa2-33f1-9408-35e54c1f70ee",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Shell Process Initiation: Potential Injection Vulnerability Detected\" vulnerability in Python refers to the potential for an attacker to inject malicious code into the system command that is being executed. This is possible when user input is used to construct a system command, as in the provided code example. If an attacker can control the `test_bin(\"partdmesh\")` or `graph` variables, they can potentially execute arbitrary commands on the system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using user input to construct system commands whenever possible. If it is necessary to use user input in this way, make sure to properly sanitize and validate the input to ensure that it does not contain any malicious code. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer way to execute the command using the `subprocess` module, which avoids shell injection vulnerabilities:\n\n```python\nimport subprocess\n\n# Assuming test_bin(\"partdmesh\") and graph are strings\nsubprocess.run([test_bin(\"partdmesh\"), graph, '10'])\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` library, which is included in the standard Python library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B605",
                  "2c6132c8-1fa2-33f1-9408-35e54c1f70ee"
                ]
              }
            },
