            {
              "id": "e56557e9-b39f-3e8a-8796-fcbc3e1a812b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Shell Process Initiation: Potential Injection Vulnerability Detected\" vulnerability in Python refers to the potential for an attacker to inject malicious code into the system command that is being executed. This is possible when user input is used to form a system command without proper sanitization or validation. In the provided code, the `os.system()` function is used to execute a system command that includes user input, which can lead to a potential security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using `os.system()` or similar functions that execute shell commands with user input. If it is necessary to use such functions, the user input should be properly sanitized and validated to ensure that it does not contain any malicious code. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer alternative to `os.system()` is to use `subprocess.run()`, which does not use the shell by default and is not susceptible to shell injection attacks. Here is how you can modify the provided code:\n\n```python\nimport subprocess\nsubprocess.run([test_bin(\"mesh2dual\"), graph])\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following Python libraries:\n\n- `os` (built-in)\n- `subprocess` (built-in)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B605",
                  "e56557e9-b39f-3e8a-8796-fcbc3e1a812b"
                ]
              }
            },
