                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Unvalidated tarfile.extractall usage vulnerability\" in Python refers to a security issue where an attacker can create a tar file containing a series of files that, when extracted, can overwrite arbitrary files on the filesystem. This is due to the fact that the `tarfile.extractall()` method in Python's `tarfile` module does not validate or sanitize the paths of the files it extracts. If the tar file contains relative or absolute file paths, they will be followed exactly as they are when the files are extracted.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should validate the paths of the files in the tar file before extracting them. This can be done by iterating over the files in the tar file using the `getnames()` method and checking if any of the paths are absolute or contain \"..\", which would allow directory traversal.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code snippet that demonstrates how to validate the paths of the files in a tar file before extracting them:\n\n```python\nimport tarfile\nimport os\n\ndef safe_extractall(tar):\n    for name in tar.getnames():\n        if name.startswith('/') or '..' in name:\n            raise ValueError(\"Unsafe tar file - contains absolute or parent path\")\n    tar.extractall()\n```\n\nIn this code, `safe_extractall()` is a function that takes a `tarfile` object as an argument. It iterates over the names of the files in the tar file and checks if any of them are absolute paths or contain \"..\". If any do, it raises a `ValueError`. Otherwise, it extracts all the files.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following Python standard library:\n\n- `tarfile`\n- `os`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Path Traversal](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Path_Traversal)\n- [CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)"
