            {
              "id": "70c0c90d-4a37-3caf-81a2-a1a258bb3b1b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Vulnerability in URL Audit for Permitted Schemes\" in Python refers to a situation where an application does not properly validate the scheme of a URL before using it to perform actions. This can lead to various security issues such as remote file inclusion (RFI), local file inclusion (LFI), and server-side request forgery (SSRF). In the provided code, the application is using the `urlopen` function from the `urllib.request` module to open a URL without validating the scheme of the URL.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to validate the scheme of the URL before using it. The application should only allow URLs with safe schemes such as `http` or `https`. Any URL with a different scheme should be rejected. This can be done by using the `urlparse` function from the `urllib.parse` module to parse the URL and check its scheme.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```python\nfrom urllib.parse import urlparse\nimport urllib.request\n\nurl = input(\"Enter a URL: \")\nparsed_url = urlparse(url)\n\nif parsed_url.scheme not in ['http', 'https']:\n    print(\"Invalid URL scheme. Only http and https are allowed.\")\nelse:\n    data = urllib.request.urlopen(url).read()\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following Python standard library modules:\n\n- `urllib.parse`\n- `urllib.request`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-918: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html)\n- [CWE-23: Relative Path Traversal](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/23.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B310"
                ]
              }
            },
