            {
              "id": "3e002c62-d7e0-3efc-a24d-e0e85c65535b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `os.chmod(\"OrthoFiller.py\", 0o755)` command in Python is used to set the permissions of the file `OrthoFiller.py` to `755`. This means that the owner of the file has read, write, and execute permissions, while the group and others have read and execute permissions. This can be a security vulnerability if the file contains sensitive information or functionality, as it allows any user on the system to read and execute the file.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should limit the permissions of the file to only those users who need access. This can be done by setting a more restrictive permission mask, such as `700`, which gives only the owner read, write, and execute permissions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can change the permissions of the file to `700`:\n\n```python\nos.chmod(\"OrthoFiller.py\", 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` module, which is part of the Python Standard Library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
