            {
              "id": "21d2612c-ac0f-3c82-963d-1ea31a592609",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Potential Unsafe Use of Temporary File/Directory\" vulnerability in Python refers to the insecure creation or use of temporary files or directories. This can lead to a variety of security issues, such as race conditions, privilege escalation, or information disclosure. In the provided code snippet, the program is using an environment variable to determine the directory for temporary files. If an attacker can control this environment variable, they could potentially redirect the program to use a directory of their choosing, leading to the aforementioned security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use secure methods for creating temporary files and directories. Python's `tempfile` module provides functions for creating temporary files and directories in a secure manner. These functions ensure that the files and directories are only accessible to the current user and are deleted when closed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using the `tempfile` module:\n\n```python\nimport tempfile\nimport os\n\ndir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='myapp_', dir=os.environ.get(\"XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\", \"/tmp\"))\n```\n\nIn this code, `tempfile.mkdtemp()` is used to create a unique temporary directory in a secure manner. The `prefix` argument is used to give the directory a recognizable name, and the `dir` argument is used to specify the directory in which the temporary directory should be created.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following Python standard library modules:\n\n- `os`\n- `tempfile`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-377: Insecure Temporary File](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/377.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B108"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d3a91fc0-1e79-3378-8079-77dd2304d6d2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `os.chmod(script, 0o755)` function in Python sets the permissions of the file specified by `script` to `755`. This means that the owner of the file has read, write, and execute permissions, while the group and others have read and execute permissions. This can lead to a file permission vulnerability if the script contains sensitive information or functionality, as it allows any user on the system to read and execute the script.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should limit the permissions of the script as much as possible without hindering its functionality. If the script does not need to be read or executed by other users, you should remove their permissions. If the script does not need to be modified by the owner, you should remove the owner's write permission.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```python\nimport os\n\n# Set the permissions of the script to read and execute for the owner only\nos.chmod(script, 0o500)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` module, which is included in the Python Standard Library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
