            {
              "id": "3cce71c2-8757-3776-853c-106b8ceaf312",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Vulnerability in URL Audit for Permitted Schemes\" in Python refers to a situation where an application does not properly validate the scheme of a URL before using it to perform actions. This can lead to various security issues such as remote file inclusion (RFI), local file inclusion (LFI), and server-side request forgery (SSRF). In the provided code, the application opens a URL without checking its scheme, which can lead to the mentioned security issues if an attacker can control the URL.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the scheme of a URL before using it. Only allow URLs with safe schemes such as `http`, `https`, etc. Avoid using `file`, `data`, and other potentially dangerous schemes. If possible, use a library or a built-in function that can validate URLs for you.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to validate the scheme of a URL in Python:\n\n```python\nfrom urllib.parse import urlparse\nimport urllib.request\n\ndef open_url(url):\n    scheme = urlparse(url).scheme\n    if scheme not in ['http', 'https']:\n        raise ValueError('Invalid URL scheme')\n    return urllib.request.urlopen(url).read()\n\ndocument = open_url(url)\n```\n\nIn this code, the `open_url` function checks the scheme of the URL before opening it. If the scheme is not `http` or `https`, it raises a `ValueError`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following Python standard library modules:\n\n- `urllib.parse`\n- `urllib.request`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-918: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html)\n\nPlease note that the links are valid and active as of the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B310"
                ]
              }
            },
