              "id": "9f16cc22-9fbf-3cd9-a135-389a350a0e78",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `os.chmod(script, 0o755)` function in Python sets the permissions of the file specified by `script` to `755`. This means that the owner of the file has read, write, and execute permissions, while the group and others have read and execute permissions. This can lead to a file permission vulnerability if the script contains sensitive information or functionality, as it allows any user on the system to read and execute the script.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should limit the permissions of the script as much as possible without hindering its functionality. If the script does not need to be read or executed by other users, you should remove their permissions. If the script does not need to be modified by the owner, you should remove the owner's write permission.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```python\nimport os\n\n# Set the permissions of the script to read and execute for the owner only\nos.chmod(script, 0o500)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` module, which is included in the Python Standard Library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc6bfb11-a252-329d-9adc-0b2751c33825",
              "name": "resources.rules.dlint-catastrophic-redos",
