            {
              "id": "ba67f143-946f-31d9-ae5a-bb8496cfb7c8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `os.chmod(\"run_model.py\", 0o755)` command in Python sets the permissions of the file `run_model.py` to `755` in octal, which translates to `rwxr-xr-x` in symbolic notation. This means that the owner of the file has read, write, and execute permissions, while the group and others have only read and execute permissions. This can be a security vulnerability if the script contains sensitive information or operations, as it allows any user to read and execute the script.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should limit the permissions of the file to only those users who need access. This can be done by setting a more restrictive permission mask, such as `700` (only the owner can read, write, and execute), or by managing user groups and permissions at the operating system level.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nos.chmod(\"run_model.py\", 0o700)\n```\n\nThis will set the permissions of `run_model.py` so that only the owner can read, write, and execute the file.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` module, which is part of the Python Standard Library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
