            {
              "id": "59a276ed-03dc-3488-8583-5affc0a0a245",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `os.chmod` function in Python is used to change the permissions of a file or directory. The `0o755` permission setting allows the owner of the file to read, write, and execute the file, while others can only read and execute it. This can lead to a file vulnerability if the file contains sensitive data or functionality. An attacker with read or execute permissions can potentially exploit this to gain unauthorized access or perform unauthorized actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should limit the permissions of the file as much as possible. The principle of least privilege should be applied, where each user or process should have the minimal privileges necessary to perform its function. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of `0o755`, you could use `0o700` to restrict the permissions to the owner only:\n\n```python\nos.chmod(f\"{script_name}.sh\", 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` library, which is a built-in Python library and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f2deeb73-196d-36bd-8a5e-44614acb5546",
              "name": "resources.rules.dlint-catastrophic-redos",
              "shortDescription": {
