              "id": "15f6c6d1-9811-346c-bc50-ca73b421c7fc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Potential Temp File/Directory Security Risk\" vulnerability in Python refers to the insecure usage of temporary files or directories. This can lead to several security issues such as race conditions, information disclosure, or even code execution. In the provided code snippet, the application is using a predictable name for a temporary file in a shared location (`/dev/shm` or `/tmp`), which can be exploited by an attacker to perform unauthorized actions.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n- Use unpredictable names for temporary files or directories. Python's `tempfile` module can help with this.\n- Use dedicated, non-shared locations for temporary files or directories.\n- Set appropriate permissions for temporary files or directories to restrict unauthorized access.\n- Delete temporary files or directories as soon as they are no longer needed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet using Python's `tempfile` module:\n\n```python\nimport tempfile\nimport sys\n\nplatform_tmp_default = tempfile.mkdtemp()\n```\n\nIn this code, `tempfile.mkdtemp()` creates a unique temporary directory in the most secure manner possible. There are no race conditions in the directory’s creation. The directory is readable, writable, and searchable only by the creating user.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the `sys` module, which is a built-in Python module and does not need to be installed separately.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-377: Insecure Temporary File](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/377.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B108",
                  "15f6c6d1-9811-346c-bc50-ca73b421c7fc"
                ]
              }
            },
