            {
              "id": "a77f65d8-6107-3367-bc98-3190cefb2ad8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `os.chmod(filename, 0o755)` function in Python is used to change the permissions of a file. The `0o755` setting gives the owner of the file read, write, and execute permissions, while others only get read and execute permissions. This can lead to a file permission vulnerability if sensitive data is stored in the file, as it allows any user to read and execute the file.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should limit the permissions of the file to only what is necessary. If other users do not need to read or execute the file, you should not give them those permissions. You should also consider using access control lists (ACLs) or other security controls to further restrict access to the file.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `os.chmod(filename, 0o755)`, you could use `os.chmod(filename, 0o700)` to give only the owner read, write, and execute permissions, and no permissions to others.\n\n```python\nimport os\n\n# Incorrect: Gives others read and execute permissions\nos.chmod(filename, 0o755)\n\n# Correct: Gives only the owner read, write, and execute permissions\nos.chmod(filename, 0o700)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` library, which is included in the standard Python library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
