            {
              "id": "2cc66eda-08e8-3638-9351-3be64e7f7ab9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability arises when a Python script sets a permissive mask (0o775) on an executable script file using the `os.chmod()` function. This mask allows the owner and the group to read, write, and execute the file, while others can only read and execute it. This can lead to unauthorized modification or execution of the script by malicious users, leading to potential security breaches.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to set stricter permissions on the file. The permissions should be set in a way that only authorized users have the necessary access. Avoid giving write permissions to users who do not need it. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of setting the mask to 0o775, you can set it to 0o755, which allows only the owner to write to the file:\n\n```python\nos.chmod(execscript, 0o755)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `os` module, which is a built-in module in Python and does not require any additional installation.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "B103"
                ]
              }
            },
